{"id":"f37eadbf-c036-4af3-b035-3ed6e6248460","object":"chat.completion","created":1791223847,"model":"deepseek-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{
  "anchor": {
    "description": "A potted plant on a windowsill that stays healthy for months even though nobody ever puts food into its soil.",
    "source": "new"
  },
  "cards": [
    {
      "title": "Plants make their own food",
      "prereqs": [
        "plants have roots, stems and leaves",
        "living things need food to grow"
      ],
      "misconceptions": [
        {
          "id": "m_soil_food",
          "description": "Plants get their food ready-made from the soil, and roots work like a mouth taking in food.",
          "statement": "The plant eats food from the soil through its roots.",
          "source": "everyday talk about feeding plants and 'plant food' products"
        },
        {
          "id": "m_light_warmth",
          "description": "Light only keeps the plant warm or helps it grow taller; it is not the energy source for making food.",
          "statement": "Light just keeps the plant warm, like a blanket — it isn't part of the food.",
          "source": "confusing light with heat and growth conditions"
        },
        {
          "id": "m_leaves_decorative",
          "description": "Leaves are just the plant's outer decoration, and the real business of getting food happens in the roots.",
          "statement": "The roots do the important food job; leaves are just there to look nice.",
          "source": "roots are visibly in contact with soil, so learners assign them the feeding role"
        }
      ],
      "opens_by": "A potted plant that thrives on a windowsill with no food added to its soil — so where does its food come from?"
    }
  ],
  "selected_slots": [
    "orient",
    "explain",
    "contrast",
    "confront",
    "check"
  ],
  "flow_rationale": "Starts with the recommended orient so the learner holds a concrete puzzling case, then explain builds the idea that plants make their own food using light. Contrast follows immediately so the boundary between 'absorbing food' and 'making food' is visible while the idea is fresh. Confront is inserted because three confident wrong beliefs (soil-as-food, light-as-warmth, roots-do-the-work) need to be named and broken on cases; correction by explanation alone will not dislodge them. Check stays locked and final as the shared diagnostic.",
  "flow_departures": [
    {
      "operation": "insert",
      "from_slot": null,
      "to_slot": "confront",
      "reason": "The three misconceptions are belief-shaped and learner-attractive, so the sequence needs a dedicated slot to name each wrong belief, show why it feels right, then show it failing."
    }
  ],
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "A plant that never gets fed",
      "transition_note": null
    },
    {
      "title": "Plants make their own food using light",
      "transition_note": "The puzzling plant from the window sets up the idea the learner now needs: what the plant is actually doing instead of eating soil."
    },
    {
      "title": "Making food versus taking food in",
      "transition_note": "With the idea stated, a case and a near-miss sit side by side so the learner sees exactly where the boundary lies."
    },
    {
      "title": "Why 'soil food', 'light as warmth' and 'roots do it' feel right — and fail",
      "transition_note": "The boundary cases expose beliefs the learner may still hold, so each wrong belief is named and broken on a case before checking."
    },
    {
      "title": "Checking the idea",
      "transition_note": "Everything the learner has built about plants making their own food is now tested."
    }
  ],
  "deviation_request": null,
  "objective_concern": null
}"},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":3466,"completion_tokens":850,"total_tokens":4316,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1408},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1408,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":2058},"system_fingerprint":"aeb56401ca74e127821c4f9126dcb669"}

{"id":"066a7e88-f9c1-47d9-87e4-d1ef69ce4480","object":"chat.completion","created":1791224077,"model":"deepseek-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{
  "anchor": {
    "description": "A potted bean seedling on a classroom windowsill that has grown tall and green next to an identical seedling kept in a dark cupboard, which is pale and spindly — a concrete, observable contrast the learner can hold while identifying light, water, air, and nutrients as needs.",
    "source": "new"
  },
  "cards": [
    {
      "title": "Plant Needs for Growth",
      "prereqs": [
        "plants have roots, stems and leaves",
        "living things need food to grow"
      ],
      "misconceptions": [
        {
          "id": "m1",
          "description": "Soil is the plant's food; the plant eats soil through its roots.",
          "statement": "The plant gets its food from the soil — the soil is what it eats.",
          "source": "common primary-level belief that roots 'eat' soil"
        },
        {
          "id": "m2",
          "description": "Light is only for warmth; a plant grows because it is kept warm, not because it uses light.",
          "statement": "Plants grow faster in the light just because it is warmer there.",
          "source": "conflating light with heat"
        },
        {
          "id": "m3",
          "description": "Air is not a need; only water and soil matter, since air is everywhere and 'doesn't feed anything'.",
          "statement": "Plants only need water and soil — air doesn't do anything for them.",
          "source": "air treated as inert background rather than a need"
        }
      ],
      "opens_by": "Present the two seedlings — one on the sunny windowsill, one in the dark cupboard — and ask the learner to say what the cupboard plant is missing and how they would find out."
    }
  ],
  "selected_slots": [
    "orient",
    "organise",
    "guided",
    "independent",
    "check"
  ],
  "flow_rationale": "The recommended sequence fits this factual objective: orient with the windowsill/cupboard seedling contrast to give a concrete situation to hold, organise the four needs (light, water, air, nutrients) into a retrievable structure rather than a list, then guided and independent practice on identifying which need is missing or supplied, closing with the locked shared diagnostic. No departure from the recommendation is needed.",
  "flow_departures": [],
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "Two Seedlings, One Question",
      "transition_note": null
    },
    {
      "title": "Sorting the Four Needs",
      "transition_note": "Having seen the cupboard plant fail to thrive, the learner now needs a structure for the four things plants need so each need can be recalled and checked against a case."
    },
    {
      "title": "Spotting the Missing Need",
      "transition_note": "With the four needs organised, the learner now applies that structure to supplied cases with support still present."
    },
    {
      "title": "Deciding on Their Own",
      "transition_note": "Support is now removed so the learner identifies the missing or supplied need unaided."
    },
    {
      "title": "Shared Diagnostic",
      "transition_note": "The practice is complete; the lesson closes with the shared diagnostic written from the card only."
    }
  ],
  "deviation_request": null,
  "objective_concern": null
}"},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":3345,"completion_tokens":739,"total_tokens":4084,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1408},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1408,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":1937},"system_fingerprint":"aeb56401ca74e127821c4f9126dcb669"}

{"id":"fe4dbd90-dac6-41c1-93a9-d7e3748335ed","object":"chat.completion","created":1791224083,"model":"deepseek-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{
  "items": [
    {
      "prompt_text": "A student grows two bean plants in identical pots of soil, waters them equally, and covers one with a clear glass jar that blocks no sunlight but has a small fan circulating fresh air in and out of the jar. After three weeks, both plants look equally green and healthy. Which conclusion best fits the setup?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "a",
          "text": "The plant in the jar also received air, so air is a need that both plants met.",
          "correct": true,
          "diagnoses": null
        },
        {
          "key": "b",
          "text": "The plant in the jar grew because soil supplied all the materials the plant needed.",
          "correct": false,
          "diagnoses": "m1"
        },
        {
          "key": "c",
          "text": "The plant in the jar grew because the jar trapped warmth and warmth alone is what plants need.",
          "correct": false,
          "diagnoses": "m2"
        },
        {
          "key": "d",
          "text": "The plant in the jar grew without any special gas, so air is not a need for growth.",
          "correct": false,
          "diagnoses": "m3"
        }
      ],
      "expected_answer": "a. Because the fan supplied fresh air, this setup does not test air as a need; the healthy growth is consistent with air being one of the plant's needs that was met (along with light, water and nutrients)."
    },
    {
      "prompt_text": "A student places one bean seedling in a dark cupboard with water, soil, and air, and places an identical seedling on a sunny windowsill with the same water, soil, and air. After two weeks, the cupboard seedling is pale and stunted while the windowsill seedling is green and tall. Which explanation is most consistent with how plants use their surroundings?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "a",
          "text": "The windowsill plant used light as a need for making its own food, not just for warmth.",
          "correct": true,
          "diagnoses": null
        },
        {
          "key": "b",
          "text": "The windowsill plant grew because it was warmer there, and heat is the only thing plants take from light.",
          "correct": false,
          "diagnoses": "m2"
        },
        {
          "key": "c",
          "text": "The cupboard plant failed because it could not reach food in the soil without sunlight to warm the soil.",
          "correct": false,
          "diagnoses": "m1"
        },
        {
          "key": "d",
          "text": "The cupboard plant failed because the cupboard lacked moving air, unlike a windowsill.",
          "correct": false,
          "diagnoses": "m3"
        }
      ],
      "expected_answer": "a. Light is a plant need because plants use light energy to make food; the dark seedling could not do this and became pale and stunted."
    },
    {
      "prompt_text": "A greenhouse manager seals a well-lit, well-watered, nutrient-rich chamber so no outside air can enter. Inside, plants use up carbon dioxide and growth slows even though light, water, and minerals are plentiful. What does this show about plant needs?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "a",
          "text": "Carbon dioxide from air is a need for growth, and removing it limits growth even with other needs met.",
          "correct": true,
          "diagnoses": null
        },
        {
          "key": "b",
          "text": "Air matters only as a source of oxygen for roots, so carbon dioxide removal should not limit growth.",
          "correct": false,
          "diagnoses": "m3"
        },
        {
          "key": "c",
          "text": "Soil nutrients are the only materials plants need, so low carbon dioxide should have no effect when nutrients remain.",
          "correct": false,
          "diagnoses": "m1"
        },
        {
          "key": "d",
          "text": "Warmth from light drives growth, so sealed air should matter only if the chamber cools down.",
          "correct": false,
          "diagnoses": "m2"
        }
      ],
      "expected_answer": "a. Plants need carbon dioxide from air; when it is used up and not replaced, growth slows despite light, water and nutrients."
    },
    {
      "prompt_text": "A gardener grows radishes in four identical trays with the same soil, water, and air. Tray 1 gets full sunlight and all nutrients; Tray 2 gets no light but all nutrients; Tray 3 gets full sunlight but no added nutrients; Tray 4 gets no light and no added nutrients. Which tray is likely to grow poorly because a need other than light is missing?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "a",
          "text": "Tray 3: with full sunlight but no added nutrients, a needed supply of nutrients is missing.",
          "correct": true,
          "diagnoses": null
        },
        {
          "key": "b",
          "text": "Tray 2: with no light, the plant starves because it cannot eat soil without light to warm the soil.",
          "correct": false,
          "diagnoses": "m1"
        },
        {
          "key": "c",
          "text": "Tray 4: with no light, the plant fails only because it stays cool rather than because of any material need.",
          "correct": false,
          "diagnoses": "m2"
        },
        {
          "key": "d",
          "text": "Tray 2: with no light, the plant fails only because it cannot take in enough air in the dark.",
          "correct": false,
          "diagnoses": "m3"
        }
      ],
      "expected_answer": "a. All trays have water and air, but Tray 3 lacks added nutrients; nutrients are a plant need, so Tray 3 can grow poorly even with full sunlight."
    },
    {
      "prompt_text": "A class lists what a healthy potted plant must receive to grow well. Which list includes all the basic needs described in this unit?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "a",
          "text": "Light, water, air (carbon dioxide), and mineral nutrients",
          "correct": true,
          "diagnoses": null
        },
        {
          "key": "b",
          "text": "Soil as food, water for drinking, warmth from a lamp",
          "correct": false,
          "diagnoses": "m1"
        },
        {
          "key": "c",
          "text": "Warmth, water, soil for holding roots, bright light",
          "correct": false,
          "diagnoses": "m2"
        },
        {
          "key": "d",
          "text": "Water, soil nutrients, sunlight, and gentle breeze for cooling",
          "correct": false,
          "diagnoses": "m3"
        }
      ],
      "expected_answer": "a. Plants need light, water, air (carbon dioxide), and mineral nutrients; soil can hold roots and supply some nutrients but is not the plant's food."
    }
  ]
}"},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1010,"completion_tokens":1592,"total_tokens":2602,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":640},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":640,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":370},"system_fingerprint":"aeb56401ca74e127821c4f9126dcb669"}

{"id":"5c31da19-fe64-40c2-8cf3-41d69f4ce2a9","object":"chat.completion","created":1791224224,"model":"deepseek-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{
  "anchor": {
    "description": "A healthy potted plant placed in a dark cupboard for a week, next to an identical plant kept on a sunny windowsill; the dark plant's leaves yellow and it stops growing.",
    "source": "new"
  },
  "cards": [
    {
      "title": "Light energy from the Sun",
      "prereqs": [
        "plants make their own food instead of eating it from the soil",
        "the leaf is the organ of the plant in which food is made",
        "leaves are broad and flat, which helps them receive light",
        "carbon dioxide is a raw material used to make food",
        "water is a raw material needed for making food"
      ],
      "misconceptions": [
        {
          "id": "m1",
          "description": "Plants get their food from the soil through their roots, so light only helps them stay warm and grow a bit faster.",
          "statement": "The plant takes its food from the soil through the roots; light just makes it warmer so it grows a little quicker.",
          "source": "observed"
        },
        {
          "id": "m2",
          "description": "Light helps a plant grow, but the plant would still make its food without it because water and carbon dioxide are the only ingredients that matter.",
          "statement": "A plant can still make its food in the dark as long as it has water and carbon dioxide; light is only a bonus.",
          "source": "observed"
        },
        {
          "id": "m3",
          "description": "The Sun's light is used by plants only to keep them alive and healthy, not as the actual energy source for making food.",
          "statement": "Light keeps the plant healthy but is not really the energy the plant uses to make its food.",
          "source": "observed"
        }
      ],
      "no_known_misconceptions": false,
      "opens_by": "Contrast two identical plants, one in a dark cupboard and one on a sunny windowsill, and ask what the dark plant has lost access to."
    }
  ],
  "selected_slots": [
    "orient",
    "explain",
    "contrast",
    "check"
  ],
  "flow_rationale": "The recommended sequence fits: orient with the paired-plant situation, explain that sunlight is the energy source for food making and that no light means no food making, contrast a lit case against a dark near-miss so the boundary is visible, then close with the locked shared diagnostic.",
  "flow_departures": [],
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "Two plants, one difference",
      "transition_note": null
    },
    {
      "title": "Sunlight is the energy for making food",
      "transition_note": "Once the learner has the dark-cupboard situation, name the missing factor: light is the energy source the plant uses to make food."
    },
    {
      "title": "When light is present and when it is not",
      "transition_note": "With the role of light stated, put a lit plant and a dark near-miss side by side so the learner sees exactly where food making stops."
    },
    {
      "title": "Check",
      "transition_note": "After the boundary has been seen in contrast, use the shared diagnostic to confirm the learner's grasp of light as the required energy source."
    }
  ],
  "deviation_request": null,
  "objective_concern": null
}"},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":3781,"completion_tokens":753,"total_tokens":4534,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1408},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1408,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":2373},"system_fingerprint":"aeb56401ca74e127821c4f9126dcb669"}

{"id":"54d436ff-3af0-4b7c-831d-559d7e26b245","object":"chat.completion","created":1791224311,"model":"deepseek-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{
  "items": [
    {
      "prompt_text": "A student places one healthy plant in a sunny window and an identical plant inside a dark closet. Both receive the same amount of water and the same soil. After two weeks, the closet plant is pale, thin, and much smaller. Which statement best explains the difference in how the two plants grew?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "a",
          "text": "The sunny plant used the Sun's light as the energy source to make its own food, while the closet plant could not make food without light.",
          "correct": true,
          "diagnoses": null
        },
        {
          "key": "b",
          "text": "The sunny plant stayed warmer, which let it pull more food out of the soil through its roots than the closet plant could.",
          "correct": false,
          "diagnoses": "m1"
        },
        {
          "key": "c",
          "text": "The sunny plant made food from water and carbon dioxide alone, and the extra light only made its leaves healthier and greener.",
          "correct": false,
          "diagnoses": "m2"
        },
        {
          "key": "d",
          "text": "The sunny plant used light to stay alive and healthy, but its food energy came from nutrients dissolved in the water it absorbed.",
          "correct": false,
          "diagnoses": "m3"
        }
      ],
      "expected_answer": "a; light from the Sun provides the energy a plant uses to make food, so without light the closet plant cannot make food and grows poorly."
    },
    {
      "prompt_text": "A greenhouse worker covers a set of plants with a black cloth so that no sunlight reaches them, even though they still get all the water and minerals they need. What will happen to the plants' ability to make food?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "a",
          "text": "They will still make food because water and carbon dioxide are the only ingredients they need.",
          "correct": false,
          "diagnoses": "m2"
        },
        {
          "key": "b",
          "text": "They will not be able to make food because light is the energy source the food-making process needs.",
          "correct": true,
          "diagnoses": null
        },
        {
          "key": "c",
          "text": "They will keep making food because their roots can absorb food directly from the soil at night.",
          "correct": false,
          "diagnoses": "m1"
        },
        {
          "key": "d",
          "text": "They will keep making food because light is only needed to keep the plants alive and healthy, not to power food making.",
          "correct": false,
          "diagnoses": "m3"
        }
      ],
      "expected_answer": "b; making food requires light energy, so blocking all light stops food production even when water and minerals are available."
    },
    {
      "prompt_text": "A class measures how much food a plant makes in bright light, dim light, and no light. The results show that food making slows down as light gets dimmer and stops completely in the dark. Which conclusion best matches these results?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "a",
          "text": "Light helps the plant grow faster and stay warm, but the plant's food itself comes from nutrients in the soil.",
          "correct": false,
          "diagnoses": "m1"
        },
        {
          "key": "b",
          "text": "Light is the energy source for making food, so less light means less food made and no light means none.",
          "correct": true,
          "diagnoses": null
        },
        {
          "key": "c",
          "text": "Light only keeps the plant alive and healthy, and food making depends on something other than light energy.",
          "correct": false,
          "diagnoses": "m3"
        },
        {
          "key": "d",
          "text": "Light causes faster growth, but food making would continue in the dark because water and carbon dioxide supply the ingredients.",
          "correct": false,
          "diagnoses": "m2"
        }
      ],
      "expected_answer": "b; the pattern shows light energy is required for food making, with food production decreasing as light decreases and stopping in darkness."
    },
    {
      "prompt_text": "A student writes, \"A plant in a totally dark room can still make its own food as long as it has water and air.\" A classmate disagrees. Which statement best explains why the student's claim is incorrect?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "a",
          "text": "The claim is incorrect because light is needed to keep the plant warm, and warmth is what directly makes the food.",
          "correct": false,
          "diagnoses": "m1"
        },
        {
          "key": "b",
          "text": "The claim is incorrect because making food requires light energy in addition to water and air.",
          "correct": true,
          "diagnoses": null
        },
        {
          "key": "c",
          "text": "The claim is incorrect because the plant actually gets its food ready-made from the soil rather than making it.",
          "correct": false,
          "diagnoses": "m1"
        },
        {
          "key": "d",
          "text": "The claim is incorrect because light is only for staying healthy, while food making happens with water and carbon dioxide alone.",
          "correct": false,
          "diagnoses": "m2"
        }
      ],
      "expected_answer": "b; a plant cannot make food in the dark because light energy is required along with water and carbon dioxide."
    },
    {
      "prompt_text": "A company sells a \"plant food lamp\" and claims that a plant under the lamp will make food even if the plant is sealed in a completely dark box. Which statement best evaluates this claim?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "a",
          "text": "The lamp works because light from the lamp is the energy source that lets the plant make food.",
          "correct": true,
          "diagnoses": null
        },
        {
          "key": "b",
          "text": "The lamp works only by keeping the plant warm and healthy, while the plant makes food from soil nutrients.",
          "correct": false,
          "diagnoses": "m1"
        },
        {
          "key": "c",
          "text": "The lamp works only by keeping the plant healthy, while the actual food energy comes from water and air alone.",
          "correct": false,
          "diagnoses": "m2"
        },
        {
          "key": "d",
          "text": "The lamp works only by keeping the plant alive, because light is not the energy used to make food.",
          "correct": false,
          "diagnoses": "m3"
        }
      ],
      "expected_answer": "a; if the lamp provides light, it can supply the energy for food making, so the claim is supported as long as the plant receives that light."
    }
  ]
}"},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1028,"completion_tokens":1546,"total_tokens":2574,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":640},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":640,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":388},"system_fingerprint":"aeb56401ca74e127821c4f9126dcb669"}

{"id":"dc2d4b18-4a00-4b7c-881e-c62e983ab40c","object":"chat.completion","created":1791224397,"model":"deepseek-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{
  "anchor": {
    "description": "Two otherwise identical potted bean seedlings, one kept on a sunny windowsill and one in a dark cupboard, photographed over two weeks with their water and soil identical.",
    "source": "new"
  },
  "cards": [
    {
      "title": "Testing the inputs: predicting and explaining plant growth",
      "prereqs": [
        "Explain, in a complete sequence, how a plant uses light, water and carbon dioxide to make the food it needs to grow and store energy for later use."
      ],
      "misconceptions": [
        {
          "id": "M1",
          "description": "A plant grows by taking in ready-made food from the soil through its roots, so removing light, water or carbon dioxide only changes how healthy it looks, not how it grows.",
          "statement": "The plant gets its food from the soil, so it will still grow properly without light as long as the soil is good.",
          "source": "soil-as-food intuition carried over from everyday gardening talk"
        },
        {
          "id": "M2",
          "description": "Removing any one input makes the plant stop growing completely and equally, because the inputs are thought of as an on/off switch rather than as a supply that can be limited.",
          "statement": "If I take away carbon dioxide the plant will stop growing altogether and make no food at all, and the same will happen if I take away water.",
          "source": "treating 'needs all three inputs' as a strict all-or-nothing gate"
        },
        {
          "id": "M3",
          "description": "A pale, small plant kept without light is seen as evidence that the plant failed to make food at all, rather than as evidence that food-making was slowed, so the conclusion is stated as total failure instead of reduced growth.",
          "statement": "Because the dark plant is pale and small, the test shows plants cannot make food when there is no light, so the model is wrong.",
          "source": "reading a slowed outcome as a disproof of the whole model"
        }
      ],
      "opens_by": "A pair of identical seedlings, one on a windowsill and one in a dark cupboard, so the learner can immediately start predicting and then judging what the difference actually shows.",
      "no_known_misconceptions": false
    }
  ],
  "selected_slots": [
    "orient",
    "criteria",
    "contrast",
    "apply",
    "check"
  ],
